<p>Smoke damage is one of the most underestimated parts of a property claim. The visible soot on a ceiling is only part of it. Fine particles settle inside cabinets and closets, cling to fabrics, work into attic insulation, and ride through the HVAC system into rooms far from the fire. Smoke damage insurance claims in San Marcos often come down to a single question: will the proposed cleaning actually remove the residue and odor, or does something need to be replaced? As a <a href="/">public adjuster serving San Marcos, TX</a>, we help document smoke damage thoroughly enough to answer that question with evidence.</p>

<h2>Where Smoke Damage Claims Come From</h2>
<p>Not every smoke claim starts with a fire inside your own walls. Common sources include:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>A fire in your home or business</strong>, where smoke spreads to unburned rooms. Our <a href="/services/fire-damage-claims/">fire damage claims</a> page covers the structural side.</li>
<li><strong>Kitchen fires</strong> that are put out quickly but leave a sticky, strong-smelling film on cabinets, walls, and ceilings.</li>
<li><strong>Fires in an adjoining unit</strong> of an apartment, condo, townhome, or duplex. San Marcos has many multifamily and student rental properties near Texas State University, where shared walls and attics let smoke travel between units.</li>
<li><strong>Neighboring structure fires</strong> that push smoke into nearby homes or businesses.</li>
<li><strong>Electrical or appliance malfunctions</strong> that produce smoke without much open flame.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Types of Residue and Why They Matter</h2>
<p>Different fuels leave different residues, and the cleaning method has to match. An estimate that prices the wrong approach may leave damage behind.</p>
