<h2>What We Review and Document</h2>
<ul>
<li><strong>Every room, not just the fire room:</strong> wipe tests, close-up photos of ceilings, walls, outlets, and the insides of cabinets and closets.</li>
<li><strong>The attic and insulation:</strong> discoloration or odor in insulation that may need removal rather than deodorizing.</li>
<li><strong>HVAC:</strong> filters, registers, ductwork, and the air handler.</li>
<li><strong>Finishes:</strong> paint, wall coverings, and flooring that may need sealing and refinishing for the odor to stay gone.</li>
<li><strong>Contents:</strong> clothing, bedding, upholstered furniture, electronics, and documents, sorted into cleanable and non-salvageable items.</li>
<li><strong>Living expenses:</strong> if strong odor or cleaning work makes the home unlivable for a time.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Common Disputes on Smoke Claims</h2>
<h3>Clean versus replace</h3>
<p>Insurers often favor cleaning, which can be appropriate for hard, non-porous surfaces. Porous materials such as insulation, carpet pad, and some fabrics may hold odor even after cleaning. The disagreement is usually resolved by documentation, not opinion.</p>
<h3>Limited scope</h3>
<p>Estimates sometimes include only the room where the fire started. Smoke migration through hallways, return-air paths, and attics can affect much more of the building.</p>
<h3>Painting and matching</h3>
<p>Sealing and repainting affected walls and ceilings may require continuous surfaces to be painted so the finish matches.</p>
<h3>Contents depreciation</h3>
<p>Personal property paid at actual cash value is reduced for age and condition. Replacement-cost policies may pay the remainder after items are replaced; see our <a href="/replacement-cost-vs-actual-cash-value-texas/">RCV versus ACV guide</a>.</p>

<h2>What to Do in the First Days After Smoke Exposure</h2>
<p>Smoke residue can become harder to remove over time, so early steps matter:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Photograph before cleaning.</strong> Take close-ups of ceilings, wall corners, light fixtures, vent covers, window blinds, and the insides of cabinets. Wipe a small area with a white cloth and photograph the result.</li>
<li><strong>Turn off the HVAC system</strong> if it is spreading odor, and photograph the filter before replacing it.</li>
<li><strong>Avoid scrubbing surfaces yourself.</strong> The wrong cleaning method can smear soot deeper into paint and porous materials, making restoration harder.</li>
<li><strong>Bag and set aside affected clothing and linens</strong> rather than washing them repeatedly at home before they are documented.</li>
<li><strong>Write down when odor is strongest.</strong> Notes about smell on hot or humid days, or when the AC runs, help show residue is still present.</li>
<li><strong>Report the claim</strong> and, if the fire was in a neighboring unit, note the date and location of that fire.</li>
</ul>
<p>Our <a href="/claim-documentation-checklist/">claim documentation checklist</a> lists other records worth gathering. Renters and owners of units in shared buildings should also check whether the building&rsquo;s own policy and their individual policy each have a role.</p>

<h2>How the Process Works</h2>
<ol>
<li>Free review of your situation and policy.</li>
<li>If you hire us, a written, TDI-compliant agreement with a 72-hour right to rescind.</li>
<li>Room-by-room inspection, residue documentation, and contents inventory.</li>
<li>A detailed estimate for cleaning, sealing, replacement, and HVAC work, submitted to the insurer.</li>
<li>Follow-up, including re-inspection requests if odor returns after cleaning.</li>
</ol>
<p>If an insurer&rsquo;s estimate already exists, our <a href="/services/insurance-estimate-review/">insurance estimate review</a> compares it with what we documented.</p>

<h2>Why Smoke Claims Are Easy to Underpay</h2>
<p>Smoke damage rarely looks dramatic in photos, and that is part of the problem. A room that appears clean in a quick walk-through may still have a film on every surface and odor trapped in drywall, insulation, and fabric. Estimates written from a short visit tend to price light cleaning and a coat of paint. A thorough scope, supported by wipe samples, filter photos, and notes about recurring odor, gives the insurer a clearer picture of the work required to restore the property, not just to make it look clean for a while.</p>

<h2>When to Call a San Marcos Public Adjuster</h2>
<p>It may be time to call when the smell keeps coming back, when only one room was scoped, when contents were dismissed as cleanable without testing, or when a smoke claim was denied. Landlords and owners of multifamily properties can also see our <a href="/services/commercial-property-claims/">commercial property claims</a> page, and denied claims are covered on <a href="/services/denied-insurance-claims/">denied insurance claims</a>. Request a <a href="/free-claim-review/">free claim review</a> to talk it through.</p>
